    "language": [],
    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Measurements are a foundational part of quantum mechanics. Usually, measurements are performed at the end of an experiment, as their timescale to process is significantly greater than that of the unitary dynamics being examined. However, the interplay between measurement and unitary dynamics can be studied by performing mid-circuit measurements. This research develops the tools for mid-circuit measurement with fast feedback in an optical cavity system, a relatively new technique for neutral atom systems. There are two broad focuses in this work: 1. mitigating technical noise and 2. implementing measurement-based quantum gates on a neutral atom optical setup. Mid-circuit measurements have applications to various branches of science, from quantum sensing to quantum simulation. In particular, a direct future application of this optical setup is in implementing measurement-based gates to achieve universal quantum computation.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Futamura, L. (2024). Fast feedback for measurement-based quantum computation with neutral atoms. Stanford Digital Repository. Available at https://purl.stanford.edu/py978gh8681. https://doi.org/10.25740/py978gh8681.",
            "type": "preferred citation",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        }
    ],
